1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:05,600
This is Naledi. Last month she got the
keys to her first home in Johannesburg.

2
00:00:05,720 --> 00:00:08,240
Her bond is R1.2 million,

3
00:00:08,280 --> 00:00:12,480
and she pays R12,183 every month.

4
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:14,880
This Thursday, the 19th of November,

5
00:00:14,920 --> 00:00:18,160
the Reserve Bank in Pretoria
will announce one number.

6
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:19,840
Naledi won't be in the room.

7
00:00:19,920 --> 00:00:21,360
She doesn't get a vote.

8
00:00:21,400 --> 00:00:24,640
But if that number goes up
by a quarter of one percent,

9
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:28,640
her bond goes up to R12,386.

10
00:00:28,720 --> 00:00:33,387
That's about R204 more,
every month, for twenty years.

11
00:00:33,413 --> 00:00:35,040
And it's not just Naledi.

12
00:00:35,120 --> 00:00:39,240
If you have a bond, a car loan,
a credit card or a savings account,

13
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:41,560
the same number reaches you too.

14
00:00:41,600 --> 00:00:43,600
So here's the answer, up front.

15
00:00:43,680 --> 00:00:49,120
A quarter of a percent on the repo rate
adds about R200 a month to a R1.2 million

16
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,507
bond, about R53 to a big car loan,

17
00:00:52,533 --> 00:00:56,760
and only about R25 a year
to R10,000 in savings.

18
00:00:56,800 --> 00:01:01,360
In this video you'll see why, how to
work out your own number in ten seconds,

19
00:01:01,440 --> 00:01:04,053
and five things you
can do before Thursday.

20
00:01:04,107 --> 00:01:05,547
And stay for the last step,

21
00:01:05,653 --> 00:01:10,360
because one small habit can
save Naledi more than R100,000.

22
00:01:10,400 --> 00:01:11,880
Let's start from zero.

23
00:01:11,920 --> 00:01:14,240
First, what is the repo rate?

24
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:15,947
Banks borrow money too.

25
00:01:15,973 --> 00:01:19,707
When a bank needs cash, it can borrow
from the South African Reserve Bank,

26
00:01:19,733 --> 00:01:21,880
and the Reserve Bank charges interest.

27
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:24,427
The rate it charges is the repo rate.

28
00:01:24,453 --> 00:01:29,400
The Reserve Bank now calls it the policy
rate, but almost everyone still says repo.

29
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:32,293
Right now it's 7.25%.

30
00:01:32,427 --> 00:01:35,080
Before May, it was 6.75%.

31
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:39,173
Then it went up twice this year:
in May, and again in September.

32
00:01:39,227 --> 00:01:44,133
Who decides? A small committee at the
Reserve Bank, the Monetary Policy Committee.

33
00:01:44,187 --> 00:01:46,507
They announce a decision six times a year.

34
00:01:46,533 --> 00:01:48,613
The next one is this Thursday.

35
00:01:48,667 --> 00:01:51,720
Here's the thing. You never
borrow at the repo rate.

36
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:53,680
So why does it touch your bond?

37
00:01:53,760 --> 00:01:56,280
Because the money takes a
short trip to reach you.

38
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:58,280
Three stops. Stop one:

39
00:01:58,320 --> 00:02:02,427
the Reserve Bank lends
to the banks at 7.25%.

40
00:02:02,533 --> 00:02:06,373
Stop two: the banks add a fixed 3.5%.

41
00:02:06,427 --> 00:02:10,773
That gives the prime lending rate, the
rate banks use as their starting point.

42
00:02:10,987 --> 00:02:13,880
Today, prime is 10.75%.

43
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:17,320
When repo moves by a quarter,
prime moves by a quarter.

44
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:20,200
Every time. Stop three: you.

45
00:02:20,240 --> 00:02:22,373
Your loan is priced from prime.

46
00:02:22,427 --> 00:02:26,240
Look at your bond or car loan statement,
and you'll see something like "prime",

47
00:02:26,320 --> 00:02:30,160
or "prime minus half a percent",
or "prime plus two".

48
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:33,600
That little plus or minus
is your deal with the bank.

49
00:02:33,920 --> 00:02:39,600
Say your bond is prime minus half
a percent: today that's 10.25%.

50
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:42,800
The prime part moves
with the Reserve Bank.

51
00:02:42,960 --> 00:02:44,640
Your deal stays the same.

52
00:02:44,880 --> 00:02:46,680
Even the legal limits move.

53
00:02:46,880 --> 00:02:51,760
The highest interest a credit card or
store card may charge is the repo rate plus

54
00:02:51,920 --> 00:02:56,440
14%. Today that's 21.25%.

55
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:59,440
When repo moves, that
ceiling moves with it.

56
00:02:59,920 --> 00:03:04,080
So one decision in Pretoria travels
all the way to your payslip.

57
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:08,560
But why would the Reserve Bank ever
want to make loans more expensive?

58
00:03:09,429 --> 00:03:13,109
Think of the repo rate as the
brake pedal of the economy.

59
00:03:13,149 --> 00:03:16,549
The Reserve Bank's job
is to keep prices steady.

60
00:03:16,669 --> 00:03:21,669
Its target is inflation of 3%,
with one percent either side.

61
00:03:21,789 --> 00:03:25,509
When prices rise too fast,
it presses the brake.

62
00:03:25,589 --> 00:03:29,349
Loans cost more, people borrow
and spend a little less,

63
00:03:29,429 --> 00:03:32,229
and over time prices cool down.

64
00:03:32,269 --> 00:03:34,469
Dearer loans aren't a side effect.

65
00:03:34,509 --> 00:03:36,629
They're how the brake works.

66
00:03:36,709 --> 00:03:38,229
That's what happened this year.

67
00:03:38,549 --> 00:03:44,309
In September, inflation was 4.5%,
and fuel prices were pushing it higher.

68
00:03:44,429 --> 00:03:46,869
So the Reserve Bank pressed the brake,

69
00:03:46,949 --> 00:03:52,949
from 7% to 7.25%. But a
brake on a heavy truck takes

70
00:03:53,029 --> 00:03:58,949
time. The Reserve Bank says a change takes
roughly 12 to 24 months to work through

71
00:03:58,989 --> 00:04:03,829
the economy. That's why it moves in
small steps, a quarter at a time.

72
00:04:04,309 --> 00:04:07,749
And when inflation is under control,
it can ease off the brake.

73
00:04:08,309 --> 00:04:11,589
A cut works exactly the
same way, in reverse.

74
00:04:12,549 --> 00:04:14,389
So what happens this Thursday?

75
00:04:14,869 --> 00:04:16,229
Economists are split.

76
00:04:16,709 --> 00:04:20,789
Some expect another quarter percent
up, others expect no change.

77
00:04:21,269 --> 00:04:26,469
Nobody knows for sure, which is exactly
why it pays to know your number either way.

78
00:04:27,429 --> 00:04:30,669
So what does one small step actually cost?

79
00:04:30,949 --> 00:04:32,229
Let's go back to Naledi.

80
00:04:32,869 --> 00:04:37,749
Naledi has three things the repo rate
touches: a home loan, a car loan,

81
00:04:37,789 --> 00:04:41,909
and some savings. To keep it
simple, both loans are at prime.

82
00:04:42,549 --> 00:04:46,309
Her bond: R1.2 million over twenty years.

83
00:04:46,789 --> 00:04:52,149
At 10.75%, she pays R12,183 a month.

84
00:04:52,709 --> 00:04:58,149
If prime goes to 11%, she pays R12,386.

85
00:04:58,229 --> 00:05:00,709
That's R204 more a month.

86
00:05:01,029 --> 00:05:03,909
About R2,440 a year.

87
00:05:04,629 --> 00:05:09,029
Her car: R413,000 over six years,

88
00:05:09,189 --> 00:05:12,629
about what an average new car
loan in South Africa is today.

89
00:05:12,949 --> 00:05:17,829
At 10.75%, R7,808 a month.

90
00:05:17,909 --> 00:05:23,349
At 11%, R7,861. R53 more.

91
00:05:23,989 --> 00:05:28,029
Her credit card: R20,000
at the legal maximum.

92
00:05:28,229 --> 00:05:31,269
A quarter percent more
is about R4 a month.

93
00:05:31,749 --> 00:05:35,909
Add it up: about R260 more, every month,

94
00:05:35,989 --> 00:05:38,389
from one quarter of one percent.

95
00:05:38,789 --> 00:05:42,549
Now her savings.
She has R10,000 in the bank.

96
00:05:42,789 --> 00:05:47,869
If her bank passes the full quarter
percent on, she earns R25 more.

97
00:05:48,069 --> 00:05:51,989
Not a month. A year.
That's the lopsided part.

98
00:05:52,229 --> 00:05:57,789
If you owe more than you save, a rate
hike costs you far more than it pays you.

99
00:05:58,229 --> 00:06:00,869
And if the rate is cut
by a quarter instead?

100
00:06:00,949 --> 00:06:04,549
Naledi pays about R202 less on her bond.

101
00:06:05,029 --> 00:06:06,629
Same maths, in reverse.

102
00:06:07,189 --> 00:06:09,349
But small steps add up fast.

103
00:06:09,589 --> 00:06:12,549
Since May, prime has gone
up by half a percent.

104
00:06:13,029 --> 00:06:17,589
On Naledi's bond, that's
already R403 more a month.

105
00:06:17,909 --> 00:06:20,149
So how do you work out your own number?

106
00:06:21,019 --> 00:06:23,099
Step one: find your rate.

107
00:06:23,499 --> 00:06:27,899
Open your bond, car or credit card
statement and look for "prime",

108
00:06:28,019 --> 00:06:29,259
plus or minus something.

109
00:06:29,739 --> 00:06:32,619
If it says "fixed", your
rate won't move for now.

110
00:06:33,099 --> 00:06:35,259
Step two: work out your number.

111
00:06:35,659 --> 00:06:36,699
Here's a rule of thumb.

112
00:06:37,099 --> 00:06:38,539
On a twenty-year bond,

113
00:06:38,699 --> 00:06:44,059
every quarter percent is about R17
a month for every R100,000 you owe.

114
00:06:44,619 --> 00:06:48,619
Owe R800,000? That's eight times 17 rand:

115
00:06:48,779 --> 00:06:52,379
about R136. On a six-year car loan,

116
00:06:52,419 --> 00:06:55,579
it's about R13 per R100,000.

117
00:06:55,699 --> 00:07:00,219
So a R300,000 car loan moves by about R38.

118
00:07:00,299 --> 00:07:02,779
Step three: build a buffer.

119
00:07:02,939 --> 00:07:05,899
Before Thursday, put that
number aside in your budget,

120
00:07:05,979 --> 00:07:08,059
as if the rate had already gone up.

121
00:07:08,139 --> 00:07:10,939
For Naledi, that's R204.

122
00:07:10,979 --> 00:07:13,259
If it doesn't go up, you've saved it.

123
00:07:13,419 --> 00:07:16,379
Step four: pay the expensive debt first.

124
00:07:16,779 --> 00:07:21,819
Credit cards and store cards
cost up to 21.25% a year.

125
00:07:21,899 --> 00:07:25,579
Every rand you pay off there
beats almost any savings account.

126
00:07:25,979 --> 00:07:28,859
Step five: make the rate work for you.

127
00:07:29,259 --> 00:07:33,259
When rates rise, check what your
savings earn, and compare accounts.

128
00:07:33,419 --> 00:07:35,099
Some pay much more than others.

129
00:07:35,499 --> 00:07:37,099
And here's the habit I promised.

130
00:07:37,259 --> 00:07:41,739
If Naledi pays the higher amount, R12,386,

131
00:07:41,819 --> 00:07:45,339
even while her rate is still 10.75%,

132
00:07:45,419 --> 00:07:48,019
her bond is paid off about a year early.

133
00:07:48,219 --> 00:07:51,899
And she saves more than
R112,000 in interest.

134
00:07:52,299 --> 00:07:55,499
All from paying R204 extra a month.

135
00:07:55,899 --> 00:08:00,139
Let's recap. The repo rate is what
the Reserve Bank charges banks.

136
00:08:00,539 --> 00:08:03,499
Banks add 3.5% to get prime.

137
00:08:03,899 --> 00:08:06,019
Your loans are priced from prime.

138
00:08:06,219 --> 00:08:11,419
A quarter percent on a R1.2
million bond is about R204 a month.

139
00:08:11,739 --> 00:08:15,739
Your rule of thumb: R17 per R100,000.

140
00:08:15,819 --> 00:08:20,019
If you owe more than you save,
a hike costs you more than it pays you.

141
00:08:20,219 --> 00:08:24,939
Pay the expensive debt first, and if you
can, pay a little extra on your bond.

142
00:08:25,259 --> 00:08:31,059
And whatever happens on Thursday, up, down
or no change, you now know your number.

143
00:08:31,259 --> 00:08:33,819
If this helped,
subscribe to Money From Zero.

144
00:08:34,059 --> 00:08:37,899
New Shorts every day, and a
full guide like this every week.

145
00:08:37,939 --> 00:08:40,779
This is general information,
not financial advice.

